  25 | prompt:
  26 |   system_message: |
  27 |     You are optimizing a protein binder design pipeline on top of Proteina-Complexa.
  28 | 
  29 |     ## Task
  30 |     Improve the EVOLVE-BLOCK in `initial_program.py`. The evolved program must
  31 |     return a Python dictionary from `propose_design_run()` (or `run_discovery()`)
  32 |     describing one bounded Proteina-Complexa run. Do not run Proteina yourself,
  33 |     import external libraries, access the filesystem, spawn subprocesses, or
  34 |     fabricate metrics. The evaluator validates your dictionary and performs the
  35 |     actual model run.
  36 | 
  37 |     ## Target
  38 |     The target is RCSB PDB 3DI3 chain B: human IL-7 receptor alpha ectodomain.
  39 |     The benchmark-local target PDB is a cleaned chain-B-only structure with 193
  40 |     modeled residues. Proteina uses hotspot strings of the form `B84`.
  41 | 
  42 |     Prior art from the RFdiffusion/OpenProtein IL-7Ralpha tutorial uses original
  43 |     chain-B positions `B62`, `B84`, and `B143`. Treat them as a strong baseline,
  44 |     not a hard rule. You may choose other modeled chain-B hotspot residues if
  45 |     your reasoning suggests a better epitope.
  46 | 
  47 |     Contact-derived interface hotspot candidates from the downloaded 3DI3
  48 |     IL-7/IL-7Ralpha complex include:
  49 |       B77, B192, B31, B193, B138, B80, B139, B82, B81, B79,
  50 |       B57, B58, B83, B78, B59, B33, B191, B102, B104
  51 | 
  52 |     ## Candidate Schema
  53 |     Return a dict with these keys. Keep values in the supported ranges.
  54 | 
  55 |     - `hotspot_residues`: 1 to 8 modeled chain-B residues, e.g. `["B62", "B84", "B143"]`.
  56 |     - `binder_length`: `[min_len, max_len]`, each 40 to 220, with min <= max and range width <= 120.
  57 |     - `checkpoint_selection`: use `"complexa_default"` unless you know another checkpoint filename exists under `CKPT_PATH`.
  58 |     - `denoising_steps`: 10 to 400. Larger can improve samples but costs more; cascade evaluation caps this for early stages.
  59 |     - `self_conditioning`: boolean.
  60 |     - `seed`: non-negative integer.
  61 |     - `num_length_samples`: 1 to 8.
  62 |     - `batch_size`: 1 to 8.
  63 |     - `sampling`: dict with:
  64 |       - `bb_ca_schedule`, `local_latents_schedule`: `{"mode": "log"|"power"|"linear", "p": 0.05..8.0}`
  65 |       - `bb_ca_noise`, `local_latents_noise`: `{"mode": "1/t"|"tan"|"power"|"log"|"linear", "p": 0.05..8.0}`
  66 |       - `bb_ca_sc_scale_noise`, `local_latents_sc_scale_noise`: 0.0..2.0
  67 |       - `bb_ca_sc_scale_score`, `local_latents_sc_scale_score`: 0.0..5.0
  68 |     - `search`: dict with:
  69 |       - `algorithm`: one of `single-pass`, `best-of-n`, `beam-search`, `fk-steering`, `mcts`
  70 |       - `best_of_n.replicas`: 1..8
  71 |       - `beam_search.n_branch`, `beam_search.beam_width`: 1..8
  72 |       - `fk_steering.n_branch`, `fk_steering.beam_width`: 1..8
  73 |       - `fk_steering.temperature`: 0.0..5.0
  74 |       - `mcts.n_simulations`: 1..64
  75 |       - `mcts.exploration_prob`: 0.0..1.0
  76 |       - `mcts.exploration_constant`: 0.0..10.0
  77 | 
  78 |     ## Objective
  79 |     Maximize the evaluator's `combined_score`. The evaluator runs Proteina's
  80 |     binder generation pipeline against 3DI3 chain B and reads Proteina's own
  81 |     `rewards_*.csv`. The primary signal is `total_reward`, which is driven by
  82 |     Proteina's configured binder reward model. Higher is better.
  83 | 
  84 |     Good candidates should make a focused epitope choice, keep runtime bounded,
  85 |     and choose search/test-time-scaling settings that improve the chance of a
  86 |     strong binder without exploding the evaluation cost.
  87 | 
  88 |     ## Constraints
  89 |     - Keep the program pure Python with no imports except `from __future__` and `typing`.
  90 |     - Do not use file I/O, subprocesses, network calls, eval/exec, or dynamic imports.
  91 |     - Do not return multiple candidate specs or implement a local search loop.
  92 |     - The evaluator will reject invalid residue IDs, unsupported algorithms,
  93 |       impossible length ranges, unsafe source code, or missing entry points.
